\section{The ratios}{

The definitions are the statistics offices', not corporate finance's, so
that a figure here can be set beside a published one:
\itemize{
\item \code{equity_ratio} -- equity / assets (soliditetsgrad).
\item \code{return_on_assets} -- profit after tax / assets (afkastningsgrad, as
Statistics Greenland publishes it).
\item \code{operating_return} -- operating profit / assets: the return before
interest and tax, which is what a company's operation earns on what it
has.
\item \code{return_on_equity} -- profit / equity, \code{NA} where equity is not positive.
\item \code{operating_margin} -- operating profit / revenue (overskudsgrad);
\code{operating_share_of_gross} the same against the gross profit, which is
what a class B filing has.
\item \code{current_ratio} -- current assets / short-term debt (likviditetsgrad);
\code{cash_ratio} cash / short-term debt.
\item \code{debt_ratio} -- debt other than provisions / assets;
\code{shortterm_debt_share} short-term debt / assets.
\item \code{interest_coverage} -- operating profit / finance expenses.
\item \code{staff_share} -- staff costs / gross profit (lonkvote). The gross profit
of a Danish filing is revenue less goods and external costs, which is
value added at factor cost, so \code{value_added} is it.
\item \code{value_added_per_employee}, \code{profit_per_employee}.
}

A ratio whose denominator is missing, nought, or of the wrong sign for the
ratio to mean anything is \code{NA}.
}

\section{The change from last year}{

For a company with a filing for the year before -- its previous period
ending 11 to 13 months earlier -- \code{gross_profit_growth}, \code{revenue_growth},
\code{assets_growth} and \code{equity_growth} are the relative changes and
\code{equity_ratio_change} is the change in percentage points. A company whose
previous figure was not positive has no growth rate, because a change from
a loss is not a percentage.
}

\section{Among peers}{

\code{group} names the panel's columns that make companies comparable. The
default is the accounting class, because that is in every filing. A branch
of industry is a better comparison and is not in a filing: join it to the
panel on \code{cvr} and name its column here. For each group and year, the
median of \code{equity_ratio}, \code{operating_return}, \code{current_ratio} and
\code{return_on_equity} is added, with each company's percentile rank among
its peers (\verb{*_pct}, 0 to 100). A group with fewer than \code{min_peers}
companies gets neither: a median of three companies is mostly one
company's figure.
}

